sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch07-09> a delphi:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Rapid production of much randomness indicates intrinsic generation" ;
    dct:description "Whenever a large amount of randomness is produced in a short time, intrinsic randomness generation is overwhelmingly likely to be responsible, because it uses every component of a system to produce new randomness and has no length limit, whereas environmental amplification is slow and initial-condition randomness is short-lived." ;
    delphi:context "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 7, “Mechanisms in Programs and Nature”; section “The Intrinsic Generation of Randomness” (from p. 315), p. 321. Scope: natural and engineered systems that produce randomness" ;
    dct:type "model-of-nature" .
}

